    "id": "VSME:0145",
    "problem": "How do Coroplast's consolidation boundary and current-period-only approach shape the comparability and completeness of its 2025 sustainability disclosures?",
    "solution": "The report consolidates all relevant entities in the Coroplast Group and covers 1 January through 31 December 2025, providing a Group-wide ESG view. It carries forward no unchanged disclosures from earlier periods, so every statement is intended to reflect the current reporting period. This strengthens current-period relevance but means readers cannot assume that omitted prior disclosures remained unchanged.",
    "attempt": "To address how Coroplast's consolidation boundary and current-period-only approach shape the comparability and completeness of its 2025 sustainability disclosures, we need to understand both concepts and their implications on reporting.\n\n### Consolidation Boundary\n\nThe consolidation boundary refers to the scope of entities included in financial or sustainability reporting. In sustainability reporting, this typically includes direct operations (Scope 1 emissions), purchased energy (Scope 2 emissions), and indirect emissions from upstream and downstream activities (Scope 3 emissions). The consolidation boundary can significantly affect the comprehensiveness of sustainability data reported by a company.\n\n- **Impact on Completeness**: If Coroplast sets a narrow consolidation boundary, it might exclude significant emissions or environmental impacts from its supply chain or product use phase. This could lead to incomplete sustainability disclosures, as key areas of environmental impact are not accounted for.\n  \n- **Impact on Comparability**: A consistent consolidation boundary over time ensures that year-on-year comparisons are meaningful. However, if Coroplast changes its consolidation boundary from one period to another without proper justification, it could make historical comparisons difficult, thus affecting comparability.\n\n### Current-Period-Only Approach\n\nA current-period-only approach means that sustainability disclosures focus solely on the most recent reporting period, without considering historical trends or cumulative impacts. This approach can have the following effects:\n\n- **Impact on Completeness**: While the current-period-only approach provides a snapshot of the latest performance, it may not fully capture long-term trends or cumulative impacts, which are crucial for understanding the full picture of sustainability efforts and outcomes.\n  \n- **Impact on Comparability**: Without historical context, it becomes challenging to assess progress over time or to compare with other companies that might report on longer-term trends. This can reduce the value of the disclosures for stakeholders looking to evaluate long-term sustainability performance.\n\n### Recommendations\n\n1. **Expand Consolidation Boundary**: To enhance completeness, Coroplast should consider broadening its consolidation boundary to include more Scope 3 emissions and other indirect impacts. This would provide a more comprehensive view of its sustainability performance.\n\n2. **Incorporate Historical Data**: For comparability, Coroplast should maintain a consistent consolidation boundary over time and supplement current-period data with historical trends where possible. This will allow stakeholders to track progress and make informed comparisons.\n\n3. **Long-Term Perspective**: While focusing on the current period, Coroplast should also provide insights into long-term goals and strategies, which can help in understanding the sustainability trajectory and commitment.\n\nBy addressing these aspects, Coroplast can improve the quality of its sustainability disclosures, making them more comparable and complete, thereby enhancing transparency and accountability to stakeholders.",
    "question_type": "non_factoid",
    "category": "VS/VSME",
    "grading": "No"
  },
